        "thinking": "Good — `SIDECAR_URL` and `SIDECAR_API_KEY` are defined in `env.ts` but not actually used anywhere else in the codebase yet (they're declared but the workflows use KV to get `SANDBOX_BRIDGE_URL` instead). That's the design gap.\n\nSo the plan is:\n\n1. Replace KV-based bridge URL lookup in the workflows with direct CF Container service binding calls\n2. The sidecar container exposes the same HTTP API as the current bridge \n3. Add `[[containers]]` to wrangler.toml\n4. Update the local `Env` interfaces in the workflow files\n\n\n5. Update the global `Env` to add container bindings\n\nNow I need to figure out what the sidecar actually exposes. Looking at the heartbeat workflow, it hits `POST /internal/heartbeat`, and the plugin-dispatch workflow calls `POST /internal/plugin-jobs/:id/claim`, `POST /internal/plugin-jobs/:id/execute`, and `POST /internal/plugin-jobs/:id/complete` — these are the internal endpoints the Node.js sidecar server would need to provide when deployed as a CF Container service binding.\n\nThe key distinction is between `SIDECAR_SERVICE`, which is the main Node.js companion handling heartbeat execution and the full plugin job lifecycle, and `PLUGIN_CONTAINER`, which is the raw command execution sandbox for adapters. Both could use the same container image, but they serve different purposes — one provides the intelligence layer while the other handles isolated command execution. The heartbeat workflow needs the sidecar service, while plugin-dispatch needs both the sidecar and potentially the plugin container for adapter execution.\n\nActually, the cleanest CF-native approach is to make both the sidecar and sandbox into CF Containers that workflows call directly via service bindings, eliminating the need for KV-stored URLs. The `SIDECAR_URL` and `SIDECAR_API_KEY` env vars become optional fallbacks, so the heartbeat workflow tries the container binding first and falls back to KV-read URLs if needed, and the plugin-dispatch workflow does the same for both claim/complete operations and container-mode execution.\n\nNow I'm setting up the container bindings in the configuration and updating the environment types...\n\nUpdating the Durable Object interfaces...\n\nNow I'm modifying the heartbeat logic to check for the Sidecar service first, and if it's available, send the heartbeat directly through that Fetcher binding instead of falling back to reading a URL from KV storage. Refactoring the plugin-dispatch to centralize the internal API calls...\n\nI'm realizing that reading the bridge config once before all steps is better than doing it three times, but I need to think through the retry implications—if the outer function fails before a step executes, the KV read won't be retried, though that's probably acceptable since KV reads are fast anyway.\n\nThe real optimization is to check for `SIDECAR_SERVICE` first and only fall back to the bridge URL if the container binding isn't available. For `heartbeat.ts`, the key is that `SIDECAR_SERVICE` is a CF container binding that should be preferred when it exists.\n\nFor `plugin-dispatch.ts`, the three endpoints — claim, execute, and complete — all target the sidecar's internal API, but the execute endpoint has a special case: when running in container mode, it should use the `PLUGIN_CONTAINER` binding directly instead of going through the bridge. The sidecar acts as both the executor and the state manager, so these internal endpoints are essential to the workflow.\n\nI'm reconsidering whether job state management should live in Hyperdrive instead of the sidecar, but that's a larger refactor. For now, the intermediate approach is to route sidecar calls through the CF Container binding — the `[[containers]]` binding is the sidecar itself. Let me trace through the current architecture to make sure I have this right before writing the implementation. I need to replace the external sidecar URL pattern with CF-native bindings—`SIDECAR_SERVICE` as a Fetcher for the container and `PLUGIN_CONTAINER` as another Fetcher for command execution. Now I'm setting up the `wrangler.toml` configuration with the container declarations and their bindings.",
